---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Fabric CLI AI Skills | Jeremy Hoover"
  description="How I built the AI assets layer of the Microsoft Fabric CLI, shipping skills and agents to production before it was common practice."
>
  <article class="section case-study case-study--green">
    <header class="case-hero">
      <div class="container container--narrow">

        <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="back">← Back</a>

        <span class="tag">Open source · microsoft/fabric-cli · AI agent infrastructure</span>
        <h1>Shipping AI skills to production in the Fabric CLI</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          I built the AI assets layer of the Microsoft Fabric CLI before most teams were thinking about agents. The work is public and verifiable in <code>microsoft/fabric-cli</code>, and it gives AI assistants the context, modes, prompts, and skills they need to operate Fabric through the CLI.
        </p>

        <div class="outcomes-strip">
          <div class="outcome"><span class="outcome__num">Public</span><span class="outcome__label">Commits in microsoft/fabric-cli</span></div>
          <div class="outcome"><span class="outcome__num">Agent-ready</span><span class="outcome__label">Context, modes, prompts, and skills for AI assistants</span></div>
          <div class="outcome"><span class="outcome__num">Production</span><span class="outcome__label">Customer-facing and internal agent workflows</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-body">
      <div class="container container--narrow">

      <h2>What I built</h2>
      <p>
        The <code>.ai-assets/</code> folder in <code>microsoft/fabric-cli</code> is the AI agent
        execution layer of the Fabric CLI. I built and structured it: context files, agent modes,
        prompts, and skills that define how AI agents interact with the CLI.
      </p>
      <p>
        When the CLI launched publicly, Kim Manis (Corporate VP of Product, Microsoft Fabric)
        highlighted how this layer is used:
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        "The CLI is now designed for AI agents. Context files, agent modes, skills, and improved
        error messages make it the most efficient way for Copilot, Claude, and other assistants
        to operate Fabric."
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        My name is in the public commit history. The work shows how the CLI provides AI assistants
        with structured product context and reusable skills instead of relying on generic prompts.
      </p>

      <h2>External and internal skills work</h2>
      <p>
        Beyond the CLI, I was one of the first people at Microsoft Fabric to ship skills for
        customers externally via the product's MCP and CLI tools. I also built internal agent
        skills for engineers, both for content creation tasks and for repetitive engineering
        workflows they wanted to automate. First-mover credibility with a public paper trail.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it matters</h2>
      <p>
        Most portfolio work at Microsoft stays behind NDA. This is public, verifiable, and
        was announced by a CVP. It demonstrates that I was building public AI agent infrastructure
        for a production product before it was common practice. I was not catching up to a trend.
      </p>

      <div class="repo-cta">
        <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/fabric-cli" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn">
          View microsoft/fabric-cli on GitHub
        </a>
      </div>

      <hr class="divider" />

      <div class="nav-row">
        <a href={`${base}/work/org-ai-initiatives`} class="btn btn--outline">Previous: Org AI initiatives</a>
        <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="btn">All selected projects</a>
      </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-study { padding-top: 0; }
  .back { display: inline-block; font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 500; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .back:hover { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  .tag { margin-bottom: 1rem; display: inline-block; }
  h1 { margin-block: 1rem 1.5rem; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 3rem; max-width: 58ch; line-height: 1.6; }
  .outcomes-strip { display: flex; gap: 3rem; flex-wrap: wrap; padding-block: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .outcome { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.3rem; }
  .outcome__num { font-family: var(--f-heading); font-size: 2.5rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent); line-height: 1; }
  .outcome__label { font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--muted); max-width: none; }
  h2 { margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.4rem; }
  p { margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--muted); font-size: 1rem; }
  code { font-size: 0.875em; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); padding: 0.15em 0.4em; border-radius: 3px; color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-family: var(--f-mono, monospace); }
  .case-hero code { background: rgba(255,255,255,0.16); color: #fff; }
  blockquote { border-left: 3px solid var(--portfolio-coral); padding: 1.25rem; margin-block: 1.5rem; border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0; background: #fff; font-style: italic; color: var(--muted); font-size: 1rem; }
  .repo-cta { margin-top: 2rem; }
  .btn { display: inline-block; font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 600; padding: 0.6rem 1.25rem; border-radius: 6px; background: var(--accent); color: #fff; text-decoration: none; transition: background 0.15s; }
  .btn:hover { background: var(--accent-hover); color: #fff; text-decoration: none; }
  .btn--outline { background: transparent; color: var(--accent); border: 1.5px solid var(--accent); }
  .btn--outline:hover { background: var(--accent); color: #fff; }
  .nav-row { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1rem; }
  .divider { border: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); margin-block: 3rem; }
</style>
